{"text": "An adapted program keeps the provincial learning standards but changes how the student accesses instruction or demonstrates learning. Adaptations can include assistive technology, accessible materials, different pacing, environmental changes, additional time, or an alternate response format. A modified program is different: the learning goals are substantially different from the provincial standards, and progress is assessed against individualized IEP goals.\n\nFor this fictional profile, the decision is: Adapted program. Aaliyah is pursuing Grade 5 learning standards. Large print, electronic text, contrast, verbal description, lighting adjustments, and orientation support remove access barriers while preserving the curriculum.\n\nExamples include digital text with magnification; high-contrast or tactile graphics; extra time for visual scanning; and alternative access to physical activities. These examples show why the decision must be based on the learning goals in each subject, not on the designation itself.\n\nTo conclude, an effective student profile is specific, strengths-based, respectful, and immediately usable. It explains how the student communicates, the conditions that support learning, the barriers staff should anticipate, and the actions that increase participation and independence. Most importantly, it preserves high expectations while providing the individualized support the student needs. Thank you. We are ready for questions.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-E-06.mp3", "words": [{"text": "An", "start": 0.1125, "end": 0.2375, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 2}, {"text": "adapted", "start": 0.25, "end": 0.775, "start_char": 3, "end_char": 10}, {"text": "program", "start": 0.7875, "end": 1.325, "start_char": 11, "end_char": 18}, {"text": "keeps", "start": 1.3375, "end": 1.6625, "start_char": 19, "end_char": 24}, {"text": "the", "start": 1.675, "end": 1.775, "start_char": 25, "end_char": 28}, {"text": "provincial", "sta